host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title The page that went stale inside its own commit, and the comment made executable
prompt Read KYRI and RADIANT_STYLE first, then REMEMBER and the itineraries and the top three log rows; Standfast on reds; take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; deep-read one council-rota row; send often.
think Council rota row 1 -- Air/Saturn, law and boundary: TAME_CORE, single-stranded, Conway's law. Conway named this lap before the lap knew it had one. The communication structure here is one durable human and many amnesiac agents, with the tree as the only channel, so a generated page IS a message sent forward -- and a stale one is a false message every future session believes. Single-strandedness named the shape of the fix: rendering and staging are two questions kept side by side rather than braided, so the hook stages only what it rendered.
think Standfast first. The ledger read all closed, so the honest next act was running the roster at a cold start rather than trusting it. Twenty guards, and readme_metrics came back red seventeen minutes after the previous lap had repaired that same page and re-run every guard green.
think The root is an ordering, never a rot. The block read 1,650 witnesses and the tree held 1,651, because the previous lap regenerated the page and THEN added tools/standing_equipment_witness.rish to the tree it had just measured. Two of the front door's four numbers count witnesses and Rye modules, which is what most laps here add, so the page is stale at the instant it commits and the drift surfaces on the next cold start.
think The discipline was already written and no program ran it -- a comment at the head of tools/fixtures/readme_metrics_scan.sh saying "Add a witness or a module and this block goes stale, so it is regenerated in the same commit." That is REDS %150's lesson arriving one lap later in a new costume: a rule living in prose is a rule nobody runs. So the fix is an ordering enforced by a wall rather than a reminder written more firmly.
obs The mechanism: tools/hooks/pre-commit rests unless rishi/bin/rishi is executable and the staged change adds or deletes a `tools/*_witness.rish` or a `*.rye`, read from `git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=AD`. For each generator:page pair it records `git hash-object` before, runs the generator's `write` mode, compares after, and either stages the page with `git add` or, when the page already carried unstaged edits, refuses the commit and leaves the refreshed page in the worktree. tools/install_hooks.rish now asserts the pre-commit present and executable beside the commit-msg wall before arming core.hooksPath.
obs The trigger is bounded on purpose. A full render costs 5.5 seconds measured, and a hook that taxes every commit is a hook the bench learns to disable -- so the gate is the staged file list, which is free. The cost of that bound is stated rather than hidden: fascia and the rooms-over count can drift from a commit adding no witness and no module, and the rostered readme_metrics guard is still what catches those.
obs The scope widened inside the lap, and the roster is why. The first cut covered README.md alone; the very next roster run caught docs-geode/libraries/README.md drifting for the identical reason, on the lap that added this hook's own witness. So the witness was renamed front_door_freshness -> generated_page_freshness before it ever shipped, the hook took a two-entry generator:page list, and every inbound reference across four files was swept. A wall built for one case and a class found on the same lap is the class getting the wall.
obs The control refuses to be theater. It builds a throwaway repository, arms it with the real hook, and plants a stand-in rishi that rewrites whichever page it is handed -- which is faithful rather than a trick, since the hook's whole contract is "invoke the generator, see whether the page changed, act on the answer." Five behaviors: a docs-only commit invoking no generator, both pages refreshed and staged, a refusal that sweeps in none of the author's unstaged edits, the resumption once the page is staged, and a tree with no rishi at all -- which is the depersonalized seed's case, and it must commit exactly as it always has.
obs Two reds booked and closed in one lap, the second found by the guard the first one repaired. That is the roster of REDS %150 paying for itself on the day after it was seated.
loom witness=readme_metrics render wall_ms=5457 pages=1 -- the measurement that bounded the hook's trigger rather than letting it run on every commit
file tools/hooks/pre-commit the wall: renders each generated page and stages it in the commit that moves its numbers, refusing rather than sweeping in unstaged edits
file tools/generated_page_freshness_witness.rish drives the control, and checks in the field that this clone's core.hooksPath names tools/hooks and the hook is executable
file tools/fixtures/generated_page_freshness_control.sh five behaviors on a throwaway repository with a stand-in generator -- one refusal, three welcomes, one rest
file tools/install_hooks.rish arms and asserts the pre-commit beside the commit-msg wall
file tools/fixtures/readme_metrics_scan.sh its own discipline comment now names the wall that runs it, and the limit that wall does not cover
file crux/standing-equipment.kyri the twenty-first guard rostered, alphabetical before geode_libraries
file crux/REDS.md rows %152 and %152 CLOSED, headline and span moved to 152 by the monotone guard's own refusal
file crux/REMEMBER.md the roster line reads twenty-one, and the generated-page wall is a live directive with its honest limit named
file README.md regenerated -- 1,651 witnesses over 1,890 modules, fascia 41
file docs-geode/libraries/README.md regenerated -- 38 rows
recommend keep-going the ladder arc's queue is unchanged and waiting -- rests_path leads the free-absolutely reading at 250 over eleven rungs, confer_words holds the queue's free head at 294 over forty-three rungs priced at forty-three import re-exports, and answered_by still leads by return at 330 costing sixty-eight openings. Beside it, this lap answers the question the last one opened -- the two generated pages both have freshness guards and now a wall that keeps them fresh -- and opens the next one a step further out: the roster records WHEN each guard last ran, yet nothing yet reads that card and reports a guard whose last answer is older than the tree it guards, so a rostered guard can still go quiet for a week without anyone noticing it stopped being asked.
